The desk marked #4471 as one known parser bug. The rendered table below shows two causes.
Split their engineering work and preserve the original reports. This page supersedes the
[one-issue diagnosis](/kb/incidents/4471), using the export dated 4 October 2026.
It does not establish the state of the live queue after that export.

## Count reports once, then classify by cause

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

<Evidence />

Use the report's tenant directory entry, its locale and booking form, and its channel.
`Intl.DateTimeFormat(...).formatToParts` determines date order; the browser locale and
intake tag are guesses, not the classification. `uniqueById` removes overlapping export pages
before filtering for #4471. The two cause counts partition that deduplicated set.

### Parser: the shared parser reads month first

Day-first tenants sending dates through the app or email, and through booking-v2 forms,
hit the shared month-first parser. A booking for 7 April becomes 4 July. See R-41200
(harbour.dental, app). Correct the parser to use the tenant's date order and test ambiguous
day/month inputs, unambiguous dates, and month-first tenants as controls.

### Hint: booking-v3 advertises the wrong order

For day-first tenants, booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, although its form reads the input in
the tenant's day-first order. People follow the hint and their dates flip. See R-41230
(copper.studio, form). Fix the hint to match the form's actual locale-aware interpretation;
changing the shared parser alone does not fix this path. Test alder.physio, copper.studio and
pale.harbour with the form, then confirm their app/email paths still belong to the parser issue.

## Reports arriving and reports being merged are different events

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The received series starts in April. The large July merge bar records desk processing of an
existing backlog; it is not a July outbreak. Reports keep arriving after that merge and through
October. A known label and a queued fix do not show that the incident stopped.

## What the prose missed

The old triage page and accepted decision 0001 say to merge by subject. Their review dates
are from 2024; the layout marks them stale after 180 days. The running explorer tests causes,
so its results contradict that prose. Same words can describe different bugs; different words
can describe the same bug. Re-reading a page recently does not make its diagnosis correct:
#4471's reviewed date is recent, but its rendered evidence still contradicts its one-fix claim.

<DedupeExplorer />

A known tenant's locale, form and channel must support the same cause before merging.
Unknown tenants need investigation, even if their subjects match. Do not turn an unknown cause
into a duplicate. [Decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) proposes replacing 0001;
the accepted historical record is preserved.

## Limits and next desk actions

The five reports listed in desk-notes describe one-day moves or other unusual timing. The
export's running classifier files them under parser using tenant and channel, so they remain
inside that count, with this caveat. Their bodies do not prove a third cause or a timezone fix.
Investigate raw booking input, tenant configuration, parsed date and timezone before reclassifying;
update both issue memberships and counts together if stronger evidence changes the diagnosis.

File one engineering ticket per cause, keep report IDs attached to each, and direct new reports
by cause while work continues. Confirm outcomes with the reporters instead of closing on a label.
Refresh the export before claiming a current queue total or a successful fix.

